ion  wealth accused to the left as a negative  thing but the stock market has actually  been one of the biggest redistributions  of wealth when we look at who owns stock  and economists are increasingly talking  about that so again I think there's  points to be made on both sides let me  present a different theory though and  I'm curious if you've read at all  George Lake Hoffs work where he  specifically talks about the strict  father point of view and the nurturing  father point of view and that one way to  tie together the apparent  inconsistencies that you and I have  pointed out in the left and the right is  no it's not so much about reducing  abortion period the reason why that  inconsistency exists well wait a second  why would you limit access to birth  control if it reduces abortions is the  right has a desire to impose strict  father morality which is no you can't  have sex and not have the consequences  if you have sex you need to have the  consequences and you need to birth that  child what do you think about that idea  this idea that that when we look at it  as state versus capital or the other  ways you spoke of another way is the  strict father morality versus the  nurturing father morality  among others is you don't need a daddy  you are a daddy okay if you're looking  if I was designing a daddy I would  design a friendly daddy you know  whatever maybe you need a strict daddy  I'm not sure but I would say like grow  the hell up okay you know and you know  nice daddy or mean daddy if you put your  life at his disposal then you're gonna  have to expect to get disposed of you  know so I mean I think it's time to grow  up man so what I'm trying to figure out  where does it's time to grow up fall  under the umbrella of personal  responsibility and the American  neoconservative right or what what is  it's time to grow up man  all under in this however we divide the  political spectrum well I mean probably  with libertarians like Rand Paul let's  say right 